<article class="prose">
    <h1>Privacy</h1>
    <p class="lead">What My Quick Menu stores, and why. Plain language, short.</p>

    <h2>Guests reading a menu</h2>
    <p>You don't need an account to read a menu. We don't ask for your name, email or location.</p>
    <p>If you add dishes to “Your list”, or choose dietary and allergen filters, they are saved only in your own browser on your device. The list itself never reaches us or the restaurant, and clearing your browser data removes it.</p>
    <p>So restaurants can see how their menu is used, we count anonymous events: that the menu was opened, that a dish was opened or added to a list, and which language was chosen, with the time. We don't use cookies for this and store nothing about you: no IP address, device, browser or identifier. To stop anyone from faking these counts, your connection's address is used briefly in memory to limit how many events can be sent, and is never stored. Search engines, link previews and signed-in owners aren't counted. Counts older than about 13 months are deleted.</p>

    <h2>Restaurant owners</h2>
    <p>To run your account we store your name, email, company tax number (NIPT), and the content you publish: branch details, logos, banners, dishes, prices, photos and translations.</p>
    <p>A sign-in cookie keeps you logged in. We don't use advertising or tracking cookies.</p>
    <p>When you delete a branch, category or dish it is hidden right away rather than erased, so a mistake can be undone. The same applies when an administrator removes an account.</p>

    <h2>Questions</h2>
    <p>Contact the team that runs your account, or <a href="https://4cs.al/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">4CS</a>, who build and operate My Quick Menu.</p>
</article>
